<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Laborde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Laborde was a French adventurer and industrialist who, shipwrecked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> in 1831, entered the service of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-i">Ranavalona I</a>. At Mantasoa he built a large industrial complex producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, glass and soap, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a> he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manjakamiadana">Manjakamiadana</a>. He later became French consul in Madagascar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-laborde.jpg" alt="Jean Laborde" width="306" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Laborde.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
